{"name":"Castor oil","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":54,"casual":{"lead":["Castor oil is a viscous oil pressed from castor beans. It is sold as an over-the-counter laxative and used in cosmetics and industry.","Looksmaxxers apply it to scalp, brows and lashes hoping for thicker growth. Users report mixed results, and no clinical trials on hair growth were found."],"sections":[{"h":"How it works","paras":["Proponents say it moisturises and thickens strands. Its main component, ricinoleic acid, makes it very viscous. A hair-growth mechanism has not been shown in the sources reviewed."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks and caveats","paras":["Taken orally it commonly causes nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea, and labels advise against use with abdominal pain. A pregnancy reference advises against use in pre-term pregnancy. Ricin from the raw bean is not in the processed oil."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"Phase III trial of nanoparticle albumin-bound paclitaxel compared with polyethylated castor oil-based paclitaxel in women with breast cancer.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16172456/","source":"Journal of clinical oncology : official journal of the American Society of Clinical Oncology","year":2005,"cites":1442,"kind":"Clinical Trial, Phase III"},{"title":"Solubilizing excipients in oral and injectable formulations.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15032302/","source":"Pharmaceutical research","year":2004,"cites":728,"kind":"Review"},{"title":"Draft genome sequence of the oilseed species Ricinus communis.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20729833/","source":"Nature biotechnology","year":2010,"cites":319,"kind":null},{"title":"Castor oil: a vital industrial raw material.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15919203/","source":"Bioresource technology","year":2006,"cites":243,"kind":"Review"},{"title":"An oleate 12-hydroxylase from Ricinus communis L. is a fatty acyl desaturase homolog.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/76